  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 307 AM EDT Thu Aug 6 2026\n\nEarly morning GOES water vapor imagery shows that the mid-level \nCanadian low has set up in conjunction with a Four Corners high, \nleading to SW'ly geostrophic flow over the region. An embedded \nshortwave is currently seen dropping over MN. A rather dry airmass \nin place will preclude any rain chances for today, with the only \nreal impact being some stray cirrus overhead. Otherwise, expect \nincreasing southerly winds today into tonight ahead of yet another \nshortwave that is set to impact the area Friday. Gusts up to 20mph \nwill be possible especially over the higher terrain of the central \nand western UP beginning this afternoon. Temperatures, meanwhile, \nshould come in higher than yesterday, peaking in the lower 80s for \nmost while climbing higher into the mid 80s in the downsloping areas \nnear Superior. Temperatures fall back only into the upper 50s to \nlower 60s for most of the UP tonight. \n\nFriday's embedded shortwave and associated cold front bring notable \nprecipitation and thunderstorm chances in the afternoon and evening. \nFor moisture, ensembles feature anywhere from 1-1.5\" of PWAT (or \nhigher, per the Canadian). Latest NBM favors 6-hourly QPF of around \n0.25-0.40\" over the western half of the UP by 00Z Saturday, with \nlighter amounts to the east overnight as the front continues to make \nits way eastward. The NBM 90th percentile distributions remain \naround 0.50-0.75\", and localized maximums sit upwards of 1\". High \nPWATs, the trend in recent model runs, and high-end totals lend \nconfidence to the possibility of localized higher rainfall during \nthe afternoon and evening hours over the western UP. For severe \npotential, the ingredients are in place but largely lacking in \nmagnitude. While good model agreement exists that the UP will be \nunder RR entrance region of an upper level jet Friday afternoon, the \nspeed of this jet is lackluster and positioning yields only speed \nshear. Ensembles and model soundings are converging on MUCAPE of up \nto 1000-1500 j/kg ahead of the front, though deep layer shear only \npeaks at around 30kts. Will note that most HREF members show some \nkind of linear structure developing Friday afternoon and sweeping \nacross the area. Unsurprisingly, the western half of the UP has been \nplaced under a Marginal for severe for Friday afternoon and evening, \nthe main threat being wind gusts.  \n\nFor the weekend, given the slow-moving Canadian low to the north, \nSW'ly mid-level flow veers to zonal. Subsequent shortwaves bring \nperiods of some low chances of precipitation and thunderstorms \nSaturday through Monday, and temperatures should be close to \nseasonable. There are hints of a pattern shift by the middle of next \nweek as the Canadian low slowly migrates eastward and ridging \nexpands across the western half of the CONUS; this could support \nsurface high pressure over the Great Lakes and another period of \nmainly dry weather in the Tuesday-Thursday period.",
